<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Slovakia</h1><p class="st-meta">1993 CE – present · capital Bratislava</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A landlocked Central European republic, independent since 1993 after the peaceful dissolution of Czechoslovakia, and a member of the EU, the eurozone and NATO.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Slovakia became independent on 1 January 1993, after the peaceful dissolution of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, an event sometimes called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-czechoslovakia">Velvet Divorce</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velvet-revolution">Velvet Revolution</a> of 1989 had ended communist rule. On 17 July 1992 Slovakia, led by Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-meciar">Vladimír Mečiar</a>, declared itself a sovereign state, and he and the Czech prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaclav-klaus">Václav Klaus</a> then negotiated the end of the federation.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Michal Kováč became the first president in February 1993. During the government of Prime Minister Mečiar from 1994 to 1998 organised crime became well established, privatisation was criticised for a lack of transparency, and the economy performed poorly. From 1998 the governments of Mikuláš Dzurinda introduced a flat tax and other reforms and led Slovakia into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oecd">OECD</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>. Direct presidential elections were introduced in 1999. Slovakia joined the Schengen area in December 2007 and adopted the euro on 1 January 2009.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Robert Fico, prime minister four times since 2006, resigned in 2018 after large protests over the murder of the journalist Ján Kuciak and his fiancée. Zuzana Čaputová became president in 2019 and Peter Pellegrini in 2024. After the 2023 election Fico returned, and on 15 May 2024 he was shot and wounded in an assassination attempt.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/slovakia.jpg" alt="Slovakia" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Original: Ladislav Čisárik and Ladislav Vrtel Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Slovakia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1993 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bratislava</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vladimír Mečiar, Mikuláš Dzurinda, Robert Fico, Iveta Radičová, Peter Pellegrini</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic (Latin Church), Lutheran, Greek Catholic, Calvinist, Orthodox</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Slovak</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/slovakia">Slovakia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Slovak Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2010&amp;focus=slovakia" aria-label="Slovakia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5078 493 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Slovakia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5398 749L5330 692"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5330 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5398 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5398 749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bratislava. <a href="/atlas?year=2010&amp;focus=slovakia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
